                <pb facs="0758" n="746" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg746"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112 European War 1939/140: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Norway</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Oslo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-09-25T12:00:00+01:00">September 25,
                            1939—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received September 25—9:13 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>64. Department’s telegram No. 49, September 23, 3 p.m. On September 3 after
                    consultation between Foreign Office, Admiralty and Shipowners Association,
                    broadcasts were sent to all Norwegian vessels in European waters bound in
                    belligerent ports to seek nearest Norwegian or other neutral port and await
                    instructions from the owners. This was an emergency measure to give owners time
                    to decide on their own responsibility whether destinations of ships should be
                    changed or not. No specific instructions have been issued since that time. The
                    present status, a Foreign Office official today states, is that all Norwegian
                    vessels are free to trade with the United States and all other neutral ports and
                    with belligerents in and outside European waters.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
